        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:47:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58767,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The most common site is the knee, followed much less commonly by the elbow and hand. Involvement of hip or other joints is exceptional {24061493}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:47:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58768,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "<p>The tumour presents as a slow-growing lesion, often associated with swelling and joint effusion {8319952}. Recurrent haemarthrosis has been reported {17588975; 21088622} and recurrent pain is a frequent symptom. Delayed diagnosis causes osteoarthritic damage. In about one&nbsp;third of cases, the lesion is painless. Rarely, destructive growth may be seen {20385009}, exceptionally with extra-articular extension, bone involvement, and pathological fracture of bone {21773788; 18758797}. MRI is the best radiological technique for diagnosis and for determining the extent of involvement.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-02-11T10:44:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58769,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Synovial haemangioma is very rare. Most patients are children or adolescents, and males are affected more commonly than females {8319952; 30980138}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:47:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58770,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:47:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58771,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:47:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58772,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Numerous congested, variably dilated vessels of different calibre can be seen, and the tumour can be fairly circumscribed or diffuse.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:47:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58773,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "<p>The tumour is composed of numerous congested, variably dilated vessels of different calibre, with either a fairly circumscribed or a diffuse growth pattern. It often has the appearance of a cavernous haemangioma with multiple dilated thin-walled vascular channels; a smaller percentage of cases have the appearance of either a capillary or an arteriovenous haemangioma. The vascular channels are located beneath the synovial membrane and are surrounded by myxoid or fibrotic stroma. Haemosiderin deposition in lining synoviocytes and in histiocytes can be prominent. Villous hyperplasia of the synovium is present in some cases.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T16:51:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58774,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:47:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 240,
    "contributions": [
      {
